WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.420
ASP.NET Core Data Protection is a framework service for protecting application data and framework payloads.

2
00:00:06.520 --> 00:00:14.200
It is useful when an application needs to protect a value, send it or store it somewhere, and later validate and recover that value.

3
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:23.900
The protected payload can travel through a cookie, form token, link, database field, cache entry, or other application-controlled path.

4
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:30.180
When used correctly, Data Protection provides confidentiality and integrity for protected payloads.

5
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:37.560
Confidentiality means the protected value is not readable to parties that do not have access to the right keys and purpose.

6
00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:46.140
Integrity means the application can detect when the protected payload is not valid for the expected protector and key material.

7
00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:58.980
Common uses include authentication cookies, antiforgery tokens, account confirmation workflows, password reset workflows, short-lived application state, and protected internal values.

8
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:05.000
Some of these features use Data Protection directly in application code.

9
00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:09.100
Others are framework features that depend on it behind the scenes.

10
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:20.500
Data Protection is not a replacement for authorization, input validation, password hashing, database encryption strategy, secret management, TLS, or general key management.

11
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:29.320
A protected value can help preserve a payload, but it does not prove the caller is currently allowed to perform an action or access a resource.

12
00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:32.640
A practical review starts with four questions.

13
00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:34.980
What value needs to be protected?

14
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:37.080
Who should be able to unprotect it?

15
00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:38.940
How long should it remain valid?

16
00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.520
Where will the keys live in development, production, scale-out, backup, and restore scenarios?

17
00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:51.060
Those questions keep Data Protection tied to real application behavior instead of background magic.
